The attached image shows that Nano-11 is actively transported by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) to the draining lymph node. Panel A presents flow cytometry plots showing the percentage of Nano-11-containing myeloid cells in the lymph node peaks at Day 2 (3.5%) and decreases by Day 7 (0.5%), distinguishing migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs), lymphoid tissue resident DCs (LTDCs), and monocytes/macrophages (Mo/MΦ). Panel B quantifies this temporal change, confirming the peak at Day 2. Panel C reveals that MigDCs are the primary cell type taking up Nano-11, while Panel D demonstrates that MigDCs also have the highest mean fluorescence intensity (MFI), indicating more Nano-11 per cell. These results highlight Nano-11's effective targeting and uptake by migratory DCs.